  "id": "DrugBank_DB01237 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Bromodiphenhydramine is implicated in the pathogenesis of the common cold through a multi-step mechanism involving inflammatory and immune responses. The drug is associated with nasopharyngitis and pharyngitis, which are common early symptoms of the common cold, and these conditions are linked to guaiacol, a metabolite that is also associated with the common cold. The evidence suggests that bromodiphenhydramine may influence the inflammatory cascade that leads to these symptoms, potentially modulating the body's response to viral infections. The presence of multiple indirect pathways—through nasopharyngitis, pharyngitis, and guaiacol—supports a plausible role for bromodiphenhydramine in the development of the common cold, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the drug's potential to interact with inflammatory processes, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm its direct effect on the common cold.",
